# CODEOWNERS
#
# Small team, so `*` covers everything; the extra entries are the paths where a
# regression is cheapest to introduce and most expensive to notice, so they get
# called out explicitly rather than relying on whoever happens to review.
#
# Replace the handles below when the reviewer set changes - GitHub silently ignores
# entries that point at users who are not collaborators, so a stale entry means
# "no required review", which is worse than no entry.

# Everything
*                        @vaderyang

# Supply chain: two Cargo.lock files, the license/advisory policy, and the
# automation that installs/updates all of it (deny.toml is a deny-by-default
# allow-list: one added identifier changes the licensing posture of every binary).
/Cargo.lock              @vaderyang
/Cargo.toml              @vaderyang
/deny.toml               @vaderyang
/crates/cpworker/fuzz/Cargo.lock   @vaderyang
/crates/cpworker/fuzz/Cargo.toml   @vaderyang
/.github/                @vaderyang

# Differential-parity harnesses and the anti-regression greps. These are the
# gates that caught P5-01/P5-04/P5-15; weakening one is a change of policy, not a
# test fix.
/parity/                 @vaderyang

# The portability-sensitive core: cBPF codegen (kernel-verifiable bytecode),
# AF_PACKET capture semantics, and the ZMTP client that replaces libzmq on the
# wire. Changes here need the corresponding `parity/` oracle and, for capture,
# the privileged live-capture job.
/crates/cpworker/src/bpf/         @vaderyang
/crates/cpworker/src/capturer/    @vaderyang
/crates/cpworker/src/zmtp/        @vaderyang
/crates/cpworker/src/config.rs    @vaderyang

# Security-sensitive surfaces documented in SECURITY.md: privileged capture
# capabilities, the unauthenticated unix control socket, CPM HTTP/TLS.
/crates/cpworker/src/unix_manager.rs  @vaderyang
/crates/cpdaemon/src/cpm/             @vaderyang
/SECURITY.md                          @vaderyang
